{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-115880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bc5b29fca0521e3da39993d0359e857333611c68c35e49c037ca7af031288ba3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/cd73bc9f35e167537996a3b33b84471dcb8fefe2eccb4afd21ca55a23e6478fd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=-1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/2e2d4b78d8181efc77f65af0c3dc4b3110d0203e650006a0e99d9a8ba933b53f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7e207bc990a9dfbafa8474ad13c17482e21c323833be848f4ebbaa580abeb201.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-120109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/76a0135eba2c33a3e69f9893d7c32a4cdc93e6d99825bbf6f72fb1db72b2a590.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b)^{2}.$ Find the value of $ab.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/68f424470a3a8ff98d7f738ce207c240795185e48ee527a434d04282d9023ed7.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-1)(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1,b=4,$ and $ab=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-120106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bedcab6fac21c2c178604e94f04f31bf1f423562fd653fd18ae5e0ea949e38dc.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/84b459d5682327d618116cd5d3ef331263548c754d2d8e0759e6fcb084fd466c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+2)(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=-4,$ and $a+b=-6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/d27af23ca398eba62c2ab1fe2ee2cf9bfdf736c16fdd8408d2a13e0d34dae32c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ab076b61b443e031455f07c1e976467cc3b74651bfb5fc2fb39f89a74e156681.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-120147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b7feb77818b19ab8b61471df9057a15fb3c39d6babc4aeee56a73e1d3c3756cb.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1e37d101f131279fc198fd97f03e6a35e15bc2d62622e1730aadbc316dc8d0bf.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(y+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}(y+2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(y+2)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/57ecd122ba41b296b3884ecd4f748f6b4ac4bd1d26f14d7b5166c5fe6e33674b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/e109d237419aaba5efcc0c65c2d97c8f8ded17d18f98bb0a95eba3336d224590.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/9be53ddfc5bd7a471ced46b76f6c8bb78fbc8b6831cec1689b6d98e8561c0d87.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/50b53f35a936263f72a3962e32d841dc85519fb5ef3e7e138111c4efeb38137e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+1)(2-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/92c557774a625d93c34fea181f435c1e6e544daa606a76c512bd96a7b9c047e7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/583fe0e13218462bdb4286ce456b0bcc4d43dd3772033e62a53242107a0a69db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115940" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115940" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115940" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/158e4f6067ce12d5a31cc6ee0b217a8d11a459db5883fdd0b28ffdc2bd969a5e.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y$\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/41b6dc7fd4e9a7dba6e14fe2d9fb87e7c07736ec0fc3cc111dd347b61c1075bb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}=-{y}^{2}(y+2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y=y({y}^{2}+4y+4)=y(y+2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-116150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8d004b698e288cbfa3cab67b1744bd9bf40c018267fa1f44a633019f6f047a9a.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/895353a5359d43f8f6256453d4f97a4f35b33acd1241f0a2950b9b21f6313f50.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}={y}^{3}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.