 [["math" "{y}^{′}=3{y}^{4}+1"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+{y}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/440d321dd0eb1b5687b392aac95d5d7d36e5a31d4cb95d5397f88afe229d7aa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/0a8b34730f001c218b6c380b9f5af0ff4b2ffd8fbb295f82e4961c5242051986.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-120176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y+2"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-120176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ddb340a99b8ab92d60e17184274854ce3d1b1bfa4972a79e74b8bfe9b0a81ce7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/73ba54766cd08a0b596e43cd8da93209e2548c68821e70a44418a24dfdf27a38.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -3.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to -3.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $y\\ne -3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{y}{y+3}=-\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{3}{y})}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-116040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-115479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-115479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8f023d50d11e5b6604e5fcc257e35aa4b8615e8c91f5855d10e73293720c27e4.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b6cf036f732d1cdff6f2e575b2ce082979d986416afd195282789303dcf70131.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}$ become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-116177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{(y+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y+1}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f7000c9b2360173996e462883bdf9f89af674b9f099f25a866cd95bd286d9150.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/5f87023b47e66436ca4f6caa8e8cc651e7de80ab09e346d955e1bb12eea211b6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 2.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and  positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}-1)}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$ and positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-116038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7b3aa65a62cb70ae297b57c5cfceecec8035afb621acec93d6fceabd1fd48729.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/890640809739a95a6d068f65678e124cc92d9e6d2fd86ec0380ee1f37a1c56d1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 2$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2-y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=2$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "960edeb32654fb5a0d9f13bb3bba7711fb97b239c3522a33d4acc0894ccdc5ab"} {"question" "q-116179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+1}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-2y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "q-116179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/state.json" "file_sha256" "41ef0f27a08231d6c6bee694ff8a0053a6b48c2a879d094fb48da850f22d5348"} {"question" "e-9306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8a4c773c2ccc0bbab1ebb7126b781504549a46b6f5991753866eff98f3c8e03d.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n\n- $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n\n- $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9306.json" "file_sha256" "9836421269c09c933258729683c346fa9272022fbed1dea2cacb4b02cd355d88"} {"question" "e-9306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ee21aabb9f81aed281951e5ff9420c5f2985bfbc192791d8d2bbd1f1c87de156.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9306.json" "file_sha256" "9836421269c09c933258729683c346fa9272022fbed1dea2cacb4b02cd355d88"} {"question" "e-9307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/998b30d8fb1df0c32a6ec63881ea247702a8e3888e7cd63ca371bc7dfe40ee2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n\n- $y = y(y-4)^2$\n\n- $y = y(y+4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9307.json" "file_sha256" "86e309cf45a6c4365cd167a36d30faf7fd7764af01a26254d586a02caaa165c4"} {"question" "e-9307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/fb7c6c77767368871d3b39991093906f6a4a0f94e2a83075570e22b3f0e87a78.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{2}(y+4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <                                    -4$ | $-4 <                                    y <                                    0$ | $y > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' = -y^2(y+4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9307.json" "file_sha256" "86e309cf45a6c4365cd167a36d30faf7fd7764af01a26254d586a02caaa165c4"} {"question" "e-9314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b064d9170c89eda881a1247b14fa295bae6b78d794ad7737aa86872fb5277af1.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n\n- $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n\n- $y' = y^3 + y^2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9314.json" "file_sha256" "f881c82cfe1e765141a6517d6644fb2968b94e61640d86fa2692123d773013a4"} {"question" "e-9314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/96fa01db29c0effec6c81996cfcaad1d46ffa71f2f1b2b3da0a48dc39bfb0d26.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\ny'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\ny'=  y^2 - 2y = y(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <        0$ | $0 <          y <          2$ | $y > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9314.json" "file_sha256" "f881c82cfe1e765141a6517d6644fb2968b94e61640d86fa2692123d773013a4"} {"question" "e-9308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f93764bdd958d41e90e09e8be2f52754835c67bc5a2ff9f6186454ebbd245232.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n\n- $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9308.json" "file_sha256" "f559557cfa923a1ecd5b4af8d9f4a836861e46bdbb0d158d32e6a6224d7a2311"} {"question" "e-9308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8820a0d0a4e0fc9d0089dea3c34a67bfaf619edcb7e0552d14a11b42a4cd20e5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0,$ \nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty,$ and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/example-9308.json" "file_sha256" "f559557cfa923a1ecd5b4af8d9f4a836861e46bdbb0d158d32e6a6224d7a2311"}] "usage" {"q-115466" [] "e-9314" [] "e-9308" [] "e-9307" [] "q-115479" [] "e-9306" []} "basis" 1507 "retractions" [[17592186167015 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-115466.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?"] [17592186167017 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-115479.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{y - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y^{2}}$"] [17592186166639 :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/ebef86d8bf088f7bfceaaed94999c6cd.png)\n\n1. $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n2. $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n3. $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$"] [17592186166639 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/fad2dd38b97e530e13888329b587e157.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1$.\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4$, but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0$, which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186166640 :question/problem "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/86ab6710d8a01fe1648d5b39a316ed5b.png)\n\n1. $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n2. $y = y(y-4)^2$\n3. $y = y(y+4)$"] [17592186166640 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/d4a4ac0f384c15b61fcaed3773ab2b76.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.Of the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n$y^{′}|=-y^{2}(y + 4); y^{′}|= y(y + 4)$\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$y < -4$$-4 < y < 0$$y > 0$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$$\\color{red}-$\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y' = -y^2(y+4)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4)\n$$"] [17592186166643 :question/problem "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/68e980c93a3dde61e9b3777f266b0be6.png)\n\n1. $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n2. $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n3. $y' = y^3 + y^2$"] [17592186166643 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/ba11bc7d92bbbefc467705ceeece8cea.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.Of the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n$y'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2$\nand\n$y'= y^2 - 2y = y(y-2)$.\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$y < 0$$0 < y < 2$$y > 2$Slope$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$$\\nearrow$Sign$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{blue}+$\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y\n$$"] [17592186166641 :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/0ef8e5a6169e508db61f76dfc5ec0e3a.png)\n\n1. $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n2. $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$"] [17592186166641 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/e919936fc92a4b4110ef32705d009b5c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty$.\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0$,\nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$, and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1$. Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163509 17592186163510 17592186163511 17592186163512 17592186163513 17592186163521 17592186163522 17592186163523 17592186163524 17592186163525] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163508 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163508 :answer-field/id] [17592186163508 :answer-field/key] [17592186163508 :answer-field/type] [17592186163520 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163520 :answer-field/id] [17592186163520 :answer-field/key] [17592186163520 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
